The forthcoming release of ggplot2, scheduled for release sometime in the next few weeks, contains updates to the position scales system to allow for future customization of axes. The ggforce package subclasses position scales, and will require an update in order to function as intended with the new release of ggplot2.

In particular, position scales now use the `guide` argument of `continuous_scale()`. In previous versions of ggplot2, the proper syntax for position scales was `guide = "none"`. In the new release of ggplot2, the proper default is `guide = waiver()`. Unfortunately, `guide = waiver()` will cause older versions of ggplot2 to fail, and `guide = "none"` will cause your axis to be hidden in the new version, so you will need some version-dependent code to support both. Such code might look like this:

```r
if (utils::packageVersion("ggplot2") >= "3.2.1.9000") {
  default_guide <- ggplot2::waiver()
} else {
  default_guide <- "none"
}

ggplot2::continuous_scale(
  ..., 
  guide = default_guide
)
```

Feel free to let me know if you have any questions!

To support ggplotly, each Geom* type needs its own to_basic.*() function. I was able to make geom_sina() work in ggplotly() by adding this function:

    to_basic.GeomSina <- function
    (data, prestats_data, layout, params, p, ...)
    {
       plotly:::prefix_class(data, "GeomPoint");
    }


I'm not sure the appropriate way to call the plotly:::prefix_class(), I defer to you on the proper way to do that.

More info here: https://cpsievert.github.io/plotly_book/translating-custom-ggplot2-geoms.html

Reproducible example (I included the geom_jitter entries for comparison):

    library(plotly);
    library(ggforce);
    df <- data.frame(a=sample(LETTERS[1:6],1000,replace=TRUE), b=rnorm(1000),l=c(TRUE,FALSE));
    ggplot(df, aes(x=a,y=b,colour=a)) + geom_violin() + geom_sina();
    ggplotly(ggplot(df, aes(x=a,y=b,colour=a)) + geom_violin() + geom_sina());
    
    ggplot(df, aes(x=a,y=b,colour=a)) + geom_violin() + geom_jitter();
    ggplotly(ggplot(df, aes(x=a,y=b,colour=a)) + geom_violin() + geom_jitter());


PS - I love your work!

Not sure when the ggplot2 2.2.2 version  (currently 2.2.1.9000) will be released but it appears that internal changes to the `ggplot_build()` will cause incompatibilities with your `n_pages()` function. Here is a suggested fix:

``` r
n_pages <- function(plot) {
  if (utils::packageVersion('ggplot2') <= '2.2.1') {
    page <- ggplot_build(plot)$layout$panel_layout$page
  } else {
    page <- ggplot_build(plot)$layout$layout$page
  }
  if (!is.null(page)) {
    max(page)
  } else {
    NULL
  }
}
```
